Patch 7936 (fix, online): np_psnr learns a peer's remapped P2P port from the user id in its punch, uses the relay only when GET_CAPS says it carries this player's datagrams, and logs per-peer counters once per room.

test13 field (2-player "communication error", 2026-11-27): the joiner's router showed the game's P2P socket (UDP 3658) to the
server on public port 1369. np_psnr credited a punch or a datagram only from the peer's announced ip:port (peer_at), and the punch's
user id was ignored, so the remapped peer was never "heard" and its direct packets reached the game from an address it did not know.
After 3 s both sides moved the game's datagrams to the relay because HELLO_ACK's flag said the server relays, but a server under
`-require-pass relay` drops every relayed datagram of a player without a relay pass (re-psn relay.go, relay_no_pass); its docs say
only GET_CAPS bit 5 tells whether this player can use the relay. The game gave up its join after 15 s.
libs/network/np_psnr.c:
- A punch ("PSNR" 02 | user u32) is matched by its user id first. From the announced address it marks the peer heard, as before.
  From the same public IP on another port, that endpoint is learned (seen_ip/seen_port): the peer counts as heard, two punches go
  back to it so its side hears us, the title's datagrams to the announced address are sent there directly (np_psnr_p2p_route
  sends them and returns 1), and datagrams from it reach the title rewritten to the announced address. A punch naming a known user
  from another IP teaches nothing (a stranger who guesses a user id can't move a peer's traffic). A punch whose user is unknown
  falls back to the address match. PSNR_PUNCH_LEARN=0: nothing is learned (the old behaviour); such punches are still counted.
- After HELLO, np_psnr_connect sends GET_CAPS (np_psnr_request, answered on the pump). The relay is used for datagrams (relay_udp)
  and streams (np_psnr_stream_connect; otherwise -2, direct) only when CAPS has PSNR_CAP_RELAY. A server older than GET_CAPS
  (ERROR bad request) has no passes, so its HELLO_ACK flag stands; any other answer, or none yet: no relay. One log line says which,
  and a peer still unheard after 3 s with no relay gets one line saying its datagrams stay direct. PSNR_RELAY_NEEDS_CAPS=0: the
  HELLO_ACK flag alone, as before.
- Per-peer counters for the room: the title's datagrams heard directly, relayed out and in, punches from the right user on another
  port (and the last such port). np_psnr_peer_stats(user) / _locked print one line per peer (user id and ports only, no address)
  and restart them.
- A relayed datagram naming user 0 no longer matches an empty peer slot (it was handed to the title from 0.0.0.0:0).
Review fixes (REVIEW-NAT.md):
- A learned port is dropped as soon as the announced address is heard (punch or datagram), and a room listing a known peer
  again resets its path (learned port, heard, punched, relay, told): each room decides again. Before, a port learned in one
  room was used in the next even after the remap cleared.
- The punch is not authenticated, so a port is learned only from the announced IP, only while the announced address has
  not been heard, once per listing, within 10 s of the listing. Signing punches with the PEER_KEY pair key is a follow-up.
- np_psnr_request_after_probe, OFF by default (PSNR_JOIN_AFTER_PROBE=1): CREATE_ROOM and JOIN_ROOM wait (at most 1.5 s,
  sent from np_psnr_pump) while the title's P2P socket is bound and its probe not yet answered, so the server would announce
  the probed endpoint. Off because the answer is read only by the title's own recvfrom and J-Stars reads its P2P socket only
  after the join reply (the hold always ran 1.5 s), and a LeaveRoom or newer CreateRoom sent meanwhile went out first.
  Off, it is np_psnr_request exactly: sent at once, in order.
- Relay datagrams are unwrapped only from the server's UDP address (psnr_relay_unwrap_from, under the lock); a probe reply
  counts only from there too, and any other "PSNR" packet is dropped. Before, anyone could inject a datagram as any member.
- After the first 5 punches, every listed peer is punched every 500 ms until 10 s after its first punch, heard or not (a NAT
  that picks a port per destination may let us hear it while it never learns our side's view of it).
- A member that left (MEMBER_LEFT) has its slot freed after its counter line, every peer on KICKED and after our LeaveRoom
  (np_psnr_peer_gone); a full table reuses the slot listed longest ago.
- GET_CAPS unanswered after 5 s: no relay, one log line. A peer on the relay goes direct again when its announced address
  is heard or its port is learned.
- np_psnr_p2p_route wraps the relayed datagram under the lock (the pump may close s_client right after), and returns -1
  when its send fails. libs/network/sysNet.c: sendto checks would_block before the route too and returns the host error on
  -1. J-Stars does not connect() its P2P socket (field logs: bind and sendto/recvfrom only), so send()/recv() bypassing
  the route does not matter for it.
- Limits: relay_ok() knows only this player's pass; the server also needs the peer's. Punches are not signed: within the
  learning window the first punch naming a peer from its public IP wins, so someone on the same public IP (a CGNAT
  neighbour) or spoofing it can break a remapped player's match; PEER_KEY-signed punches are the follow-up.
  np_psnr_stream_connect still reads s_client after unlocking (older; psnr_client is opaque here, so not copied).
libs/network/sceNpMatching2.c: MEMBER_LEFT logs that member's counters, KICKED and the LeaveRoom reply log every peer's. MEMBER_LEFT
and KICKED now take the np_psnr peer lock before the state lock, as MEMBER_JOINED does; the LeaveRoom reply logs after the state lock
is released.
Tests: libs/network/tests/test_np_nat.c (remapped port learned from the punch, not from another IP, the route and the rewrite, the
switch off; the relay decision for CAPS with and without bit 5, a pre-GET_CAPS server, an error, no answer, the switch; the counters;
the review's probes P1-P5 and the other review fixes: drop on the announced port, reset on relisting, once per listing, the
window, forged relay and probe packets, back from the relay, slow punches, freed and reused slots, the GET_CAPS deadline, the
held room request with its answer, its cap and its switch),
built and run on Windows by installer/tools/np_nat_test.sh. test_np_matching2.c gets the new stubs. Not run against a real
server or router (no two-router test); matching2_context_test.py (posix only) not run.

